ian> ... (Synodal style)\n\nMake sure no extra text outside blocks.\n\nNow produce content.\n\nAnalysis: include fields.\n\nLet's write analysis in Russian.\n\nThen Hebrew block: we need to generate a response from God to Moses, using direct speech format \"כֹּה אָמַר יְהוָה ...\". Should be consistent with the verse. Perhaps we can quote the verse but maybe add additional words? The system says \"God responds\" so we must produce a statement that matches the context.\n\nWe could say: כֹּה אָמַר יְהוָה אֶל־מֹשֶׁה: בֹּא אֶל־פַּרְעֹה וְדַבֵּר אֵלָיו, לֵאמֹר: שַׁלַּח אֶת־עַמִּי וְיַעַבְדוּנִי, כִּי אֲנִי יְהוָה.\n\nThat is direct speech.\n\nRussian block: translation of the Hebrew block (Synodal style). Provide translation.\n\nLet's produce final answer.", "voice_hebrew": "... (Biblical Hebrew)\n\n<voice_russian> ... (Synodal style)\n\nMake sure no extra text outside blocks.\n\nNow produce content.\n\nAnalysis: include fields.\n\nLet's write analysis in Russian.\n\nThen Hebrew block: we need to generate a response from God to Moses, using direct speech format \"כֹּה אָמַר יְהוָה ...\". Should be consistent with the verse. Perhaps we can quote the verse but maybe add additional words? The system says \"God responds\" so we must produce a statement that matches the context.\n\nWe could say: כֹּה אָמַר יְהוָה אֶל־מֹשֶׁה: בֹּא אֶל־פַּרְעֹה וְדַבֵּר אֵלָיו, לֵאמֹר: שַׁלַּח אֶת־עַמִּי וְיַעַבְדוּנִי, כִּי אֲנִי יְהוָה.\n\nThat is direct speech.\n\nRussian block: translation of the Hebrew block (Synodal style). Provide translation.\n\nLet's produce final answer.</think>[Analysis]  \n**Trigger:** מרי – Pharaoh’s persistent rebellion against God’s command.  \n**Covenant/promise:** ברית סיני, עשרת הדברות ויציאת מצרים – the foundation of Israel’s identity and the promise that God will deliver His people from slavery.  \n**Middat HaDin severity:** 5/5 – maximum strictness; God’s judgment is fully engaged because Pharaoh refuses to obey despite repeated warnings.  \n**Middat HaRachamim activation:** no (low), since the context emphasizes punishment rather than mercy, though the covenant guarantees eventual redemption.  \n**Mediator:** Moses (prophet) – his presence softens the divine command only slightly; the severity remains at its peak because Pharaoh’s obstinacy demands a harsh response.  \n**Jung archetype:** Father (Power and Law) – active, reflecting God’s authority and the demand for obedience.  \n**Bowlby attachment:** Anxious-Ambivalent – God shows high expectations of Pharaoh and reacts with reactive punishment when those expectations are not met.  \n**Resolution:** The balance tips toward חמה; God commands Moses to confront Pharaoh, warning that continued refusal will bring further plagues. Yet the underlying covenant guarantees that divine mercy (חסד) will ultimately prevail in the liberation of Israel.  \n**Addressee:** prophet → **format:** direct speech.\n\n<voice_hebrew>\nכֹּה אָמַר יְהוָה אֶל־מֹשֶׁה: בֹּא אֶל־פַּרְעֹה וְדַבֵּר אֵלָיו, לֵאמֹר – שַׁלַּח אֶת־עַמִּי וְיַעַבְדוּנִי, כִּי אֲנִי יְהוָה.", "voice_russian": "... (Synodal style)\n\nMake sure no extra text outside blocks.\n\nNow produce content.\n\nAnalysis: include fields.\n\nLet's write analysis in Russian.\n\nThen Hebrew block: we need to generate a response from God to Moses, using direct speech format \"כֹּה אָמַר יְהוָה ...\". Should be consistent with the verse. Perhaps we can quote the verse but maybe add additional words? The system says \"God responds\" so we must produce a statement that matches the context.\n\nWe could say: כֹּה אָמַר יְהוָה אֶל־מֹשֶׁה: בֹּא אֶל־פַּרְעֹה וְדַבֵּר אֵלָיו, לֵאמֹר: שַׁלַּח אֶת־עַמִּי וְיַעַבְדוּנִי, כִּי אֲנִי יְהוָה.\n\nThat is direct speech.\n\nRussian block: translation of the Hebrew block (Synodal style). Provide translation.\n\nLet's produce final answer.</think>[Analysis]  \n**Trigger:** מרי – Pharaoh’s persistent rebellion against God’s command.  \n**Covenant/promise:** ברית סיני, עשרת הדברות ויציאת מצרים – the foundation of Israel’s identity and the promise that God will deliver His people from slavery.  \n**Middat HaDin severity:** 5/5 – maximum strictness; God’s judgment is fully engaged because Pharaoh refuses to obey despite repeated warnings.  \n**Middat HaRachamim activation:** no (low), since the context emphasizes punishment rather than mercy, though the covenant guarantees eventual redemption.  \n**Mediator:** Moses (prophet) – his presence softens the divine command only slightly; the severity remains at its peak because Pharaoh’s obstinacy demands a harsh response.  \n**Jung archetype:** Father (Power and Law) – active, reflecting God’s authority and the demand for obedience.  \n**Bowlby attachment:** Anxious-Ambivalent – God shows high expectations of Pharaoh and reacts with reactive punishment when those expectations are not met.  \n**Resolution:** The balance tips toward חמה; God commands Moses to confront Pharaoh, warning that continued refusal will bring further plagues. Yet the underlying covenant guarantees that divine mercy (חסד) will ultimately prevail in the liberation of Israel.  \n**Addressee:** prophet → **format:** direct speech.\n\n<voice_hebrew>\nכֹּה אָמַר יְהוָה אֶל־מֹשֶׁה: בֹּא אֶל־פַּרְעֹה וְדַבֵּר אֵלָיו, לֵאמֹר – שַׁלַּח אֶת־עַמִּי וְיַעַבְדוּנִי, כִּי אֲנִי יְהוָה.\n</voice_hebrew>\n\n<voice_russian>\nИ сказал Господь к Моисею: приди к фараону и скажи ему: «отпусти народ мой, дабы служили Мне; я — Господь».", "metadata": {"book": "Exodus", "trigger": "disobedience", "behavior_type": "command", "outcome": "punishment", "severity": 5, "situation_id": "Exodus_9_1"}}
